german president frank walter steinmeier  has been re-elected for a second  five-year term  the 66-year-old social democrat and  former foreign minister received more  than two-thirds of the votes cast by an  electoral assembly made up of  politicians civil society figures and  celebrities sean meyer had the support  of all three parties of germany's  governing coalition  nominally the head of state the role of  president is largely ceremonial in  german politics  but in his acceptance speech steinmeier  urged germany and its allies to do  everything to prevent war in europe  the lack of war on our continent ladies  and gentlemen  is something we have become used to  protected by friends  at peace with our neighbors for over 30  years unified again  what a joy this has been for our country  in recent days and weeks we have learned  once again what we should have known we  cannot take peace for granted  peace must be worked for  in a dialogue but where necessary with  clear words with deterrence  joining forces together ladies and  gentlemen and that's what we need now  and for more on this i'm joined in the  studio by dw chief political  correspondent melinda crane and nina  hasa who is at the parliament buildings  in downtown berlin nina scheinmaier was  reelected with a large majority but were  there any surprises  well in terms of election results um  there were a few minor surprises of  course frankfurt steinmeier being  re-elected was not a big surprise after  all he did have the backing of  all the parties that are involved in the  current government coalition so the sbds  the greens and the fdps but also he got  the support from the conservatives from  uh formerly angela merkel's party and  afghanistan's party and that gave him a  solid majority he got more than a  thousand votes now 1  400  more than 1 400 votes were up for grabs  what was interesting though in terms of  results i'm sure that we'll get to the  details of his speech later but just in  terms of results was that the candidate  who had been nominated by the three  voters the only woman in the race she  got more votes than many people had  expected she  would have only got some 36  but she made it more than 50. and also  the candidate who is appointed by the  far-right afd party that had been the  most controversial candidacy max otto  who is a member of the conservatives he  got far fewer votes than  the amount of afd the votes would have  been  okay well those are some interesting  insights i'm going to turn to melissa  melinda crane now um  about the speech i mean that was a  pretty interesting speech um  it sort of ventured into foreign policy  quite a bit was it was the president  breaking the mold somewhat  he was breaking the mold perhaps with  the clarity of his uh messages on  foreign policy but actually his unifying  theme was democracy he started out by  saying if you are fighting to preserve  democracy i'm on your side and the  office of the presidency in germany  actually was created to stabilize the  then young german democracy so this is a  traditional theme for many presidents  but it's been an especially strong theme  for him also in his first term in office  so his messages on the external threats  to democracy essentially were all  addressed to russia and president putin  with steinmeier saying very clearly  russia is responsible for the current  threat also two direct appeals to putin  saying president putin do not  underestimate the strength of  democracies and president putin and i  think this certainly was the most  headline worthy probably of these  messages  loosened the noose around the throat of  ukraine and he pointed out that that  threat is not only to ukraine but also  to other eastern neighbors and in fact  his first foreign trip this coming week  will be to latvia one of the baltic  republics that does feel threatened but  he went on to join that theme with uh  the threats to democracy at home talking  about the fact that both the pandemic  and also the transformational process  we're seeing as germany moves away from  fossil fuels to new energy systems to a  more green economy that these are  sources of pain and division within the  society and he talked about how he wants  to be a president who listens to those  who are fearful about the future who  listens to their concerns and that he  would make his first domestic trip to  eastern germany where many people do  feel that the future is less secure  perhaps than the past  okay and i'll just go back to to to to  nina if i may who's uh there at the  bundestag forest can you tell us a  little bit more about how the day  unfolded um i understand the voting was  i mean we watched it live it was bit  tricky with the pandemic it's not in the  usual place there were celebrities and  so on what more can you tell us  well yeah i report from the bundestag on  a very regular basis and today was very  different from our usual reporting of  course one because the federal  convention is such a unique body it only  gets together once every five years and  its sole purpose is to elect the german  president now the federal convention is  made up of all the members of parliament  here and all the parties get the same  amount of votes that they can then  essentially hand to people from all  walks of life from representatives of  society who they think stand for their  own values and so we've had a very  colorful uh very um diverse mix of  people here in bundestag today there  were rappers there were football  superstars angela merkel the former  chancellor was here so very different  and usually it's a day where germany  celebrates its democracy now we all  famously um are not the kind of nation  that puts too much glamour into its  democratic processes but the federal  convention meeting is usually one such  day where we celebrate democracy with  symbols and rituals but because of  anti-covet rules that are still in place  there was a lot of focus on keeping your  distance from each other and also from  everybody had to get tested who came in  here and also when they played the  national anthem you weren't allowed to  sing but you were allowed to hum  yeah and i'll note in passing that while  it was all about frank falterstein it  seemed like everybody wanted a selfie  with angela merkel political  correspondent nina has at the bonus tag  there for us and melinda craner chief  political correspondent here in the  studio thanks so much  you
 

